has at most $3$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186416481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5650e0d2-585c-5516-892d-5eacef061cb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416481 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97ca7c6b-3d02-595e-9b24-0e63b350342b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}17-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (17-\\lambda )(17-\\lambda )-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +289-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +225 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429230 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429230 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3819->991. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429230 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048775 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434790 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434790 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434790 #edb/